To output a PDF in SAS with a journal format, you can use the `ods pdf` statement in conjunction with the `ods journal` option. Here's a basic example:
```sas
ods pdf file="output.pdf" journal;
proc print data=sashelp.cars;
run;
ods pdf close;
```
This will create a PDF file named "output.pdf" with a journal format. The `ods pdf` statement specifies the output file, and the `journal` option specifies the journal format.

Here are some additional options you can use to customize the journal format:

* `ods pdf file="output.pdf" journal=simple`: This will use a simple journal format with a single page.
* `ods pdf file="output.pdf" journal=inline`: This will use an inline journal format with multiple pages.
* `ods pdf file="output.pdf" journal=narrow`: This will use a narrow journal format with a single column.
* `ods pdf file="output.pdf" journal=widen`: This will use a wide journal format with multiple columns.

You can also use other ODS (Output Delivery System) statements to customize the output, such as `ods region`, `ods graphics`, and `ods style`.

For example, you can use the `ods region` statement to specify the region of the journal where you want to output the data:
```sas
ods pdf file="output.pdf" journal;
ods region journalregion;
proc print data=sashelp.cars;
run;
ods region close;
ods pdf close;
```
This will output the data in the journal region specified.

You can also use the `ods graphics` statement to include graphics in your journal:
```sas
ods pdf file="output.pdf" journal;
ods graphics on;
proc gplot data=sashelp.cars;
plot x*y;
run;
ods graphics off;
ods pdf close;
```
This will include a plot of the x-y data in the journal.

You can also use the `ods style` statement to customize the style of the journal:
```sas
